      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) improves outcomes when given post systemic ischemia/reperfusion injury. iNO given during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) may therefore improve return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) rates and functional outcome after cardiac arrest (CA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Thirty male Sprague-Dawley rats were subjected to 10 minutes of CA and at least 3 minutes of CPR. Animals were randomized to receive either 0 (n = 10, Control), 20 (n = 10, 20 ppm), or 40 (n = 10, 40 ppm) ppm iNO during CPR until 30 minutes after ROSC. A neurological deficit score was assessed daily for seven days following the experiment. On day 7, brains, hearts, and blood were sampled for histological and biochemical evaluation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During CPR, 20 ppm iNO significantly increased diastolic arterial pressure ("
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CONTROL",
          "content": "57 ± 5.04 mmHg; 20 ppm: 71.57 ± 57.3 mmHg, p < 0.046) and decreased time to ROSC (CONTROL: 842 ± 21 s; 20 ppm: 792 ± 5 s, (p = 0.02)). Thirty minutes following ROSC, 20 ppm iNO resulted in an increase in mean arterial pressure ("
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CONTROL",
          "content": "83 ± 4 mmHg; 20 ppm: 98 ± 4 mmHg, p = 0.035), a less pronounced rise in lactate and inflammatory cytokine levels, and attenuated cardiac damage. Inhalation of NO at 20 ppm improved neurological outcomes in rats 2 to 7 days after CA and CPR. This translated into increases in 7 day survival ("
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CONTROL",
          "content": "4; 20 ppm: 10; 40 ppm 6, (p ≤ 0.05 20 ppm vs CONTROL and 40 ppm)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our study revealed that breathing NO during CPR markedly improved resuscitation success, 7-day neurological outcomes and survival in a rat model of VF-induced cardiac arrest and CPR. These results support the beneficial effects of NO inhalation after cardiac arrest and CPR."
        }
      ]},
